<speak>
<p>Let u be odd and greater than one. Each coefficient of the four sign polynomials is positive or negative one. These same polynomials define c, d, and g. Node five formed exact polynomial quotients a and b: d minus c equals two a, while g minus c equals the product of one plus i and b. The coefficients of a are integers, and those of b are Gaussian integers. These divisions were made before reduction.<break time="700ms"/>Keep one evaluation ring throughout. Let B be the integers with i and the chosen prime roots adjoined, and let A be B with one over u adjoined. For a subset S of the primes dividing u, multiply its chosen roots to obtain zeta sub S. Write c sub S for c at that input, and similarly for d and g. The preceding node proves that all three are units in A, and that A modulo one plus i is nonzero. In particular, c sub S has inverse its complex conjugate divided by u squared, belonging to A. Evaluate the two exact identities and keep this unit denominator visible.<break time="1000ms"/></p>
<p>Define r sub S as d sub S divided by c sub S, and w sub S as g sub S divided by c sub S. Subtract one from the first ratio. Combine the fractions, then replace d sub S minus c sub S by two times a at zeta sub S. The result is two times a at that input divided by c sub S. Repeat with the second ratio: w sub S minus one equals the factor one plus i, multiplied by b at that input and divided by c sub S.<break time="700ms"/>Both remaining quotients belong to A, because the denominator is a unit. Two equals negative i times the square of one plus i, so both displayed factors vanish modulo one plus i. The reduced differences are zero. Therefore r sub S and w sub S each have residue one. Every division was by a unit; we did not divide by a zero residue.<break time="1000ms"/></p>
<p>Now unpack the Delta comparisons into alternating products of the r ratios and the w ratios, with positive exponents for even subsets and negative exponents for odd subsets. Every factor is a unit of residue one. Its inverse has residue one too: reduce the product of a unit and its inverse, and their product is still one. Since the first residue is one, the inverse residue must also be one. Thus every factor in both alternating products reduces to one, including the inverse factors. Both capital R and capital W have residue one modulo one plus i.<break time="700ms"/>For u equal to three, the two inputs are one and omega, a primitive cube root. Capital R is r at the empty subset divided by r at the subset containing three; capital W has the same form using w. More distinct primes give more factors. The same product and inverse rule applies to all of them. This is an algebraic residue condition. The exact comparisons still await odd-order rigidity.<break time="1200ms"/></p>
</speak>
